1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,510
A team migrates forty repositories to
another host over a weekend with git clone

2
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:14,433
--mirror and git push --mirror. On Monday
the code and the history are intact.

3
00:00:14,473 --> 00:00:19,357
The open pull requests, three years of
review comments, the release notes, the

4
00:00:19,397 --> 00:00:22,766
protection on main and the
deploy secrets are missing.

5
00:00:22,806 --> 00:00:26,111
Nobody exported them, because
they were never in Git.

6
00:00:26,451 --> 00:00:32,073
And a second question from the same CTO: a
new engineer got Write on one repository.

7
00:00:32,113 --> 00:00:34,836
Why can she clone all forty private ones?

8
00:00:35,176 --> 00:00:39,925
Neither question is about a Git command.
Both are answered by one habit, and this

9
00:00:39,965 --> 00:00:44,359
video builds it. Hold on to the second
question. You'll answer it yourself.

10
00:00:46,199 --> 00:00:50,918
Welcome back to Git and GitHub Deep
Mastery. Pull up a chair: this is Part 5.

11
00:00:50,958 --> 00:00:55,801
For four parts you worked with Git:
objects, refs, the index, the working tree.

12
00:00:55,841 --> 00:01:00,869
Everything was in a directory you could
copy. From here on there's a second system

13
00:01:00,909 --> 00:01:05,134
in the picture, GitHub, and the first
skill is to keep the two apart.

14
00:01:05,474 --> 00:01:10,512
The habit: for every thing you touch, know
whether it's Git data or a GitHub object.

15
00:01:10,552 --> 00:01:15,227
Git data lives in refs and objects, and
it travels with clone, fetch and push.

16
00:01:15,267 --> 00:01:20,305
Objects are the stored commits and files,
and refs are the names that point at them,

17
00:01:20,345 --> 00:01:25,383
such as branches and tags. A GitHub object
lives in GitHub's database and is reached

18
00:01:25,423 --> 00:01:28,587
through the web interface,
the GitHub CLI or the API.

19
00:01:28,627 --> 00:01:33,665
The best-known one is the pull request, a
proposal to merge one branch into another.

20
00:01:34,005 --> 00:01:38,581
A word on how Part 5 is recorded. Local
scripts show the Git side of every

21
00:01:38,621 --> 00:01:43,696
mechanism, with real output. The GitHub
side is a screen walkthrough of a practice

22
00:01:43,736 --> 00:01:48,874
repository. The authors captured no GitHub
output. The interface changes, so I name

23
00:01:48,914 --> 00:01:54,052
each control by its function, and the lab
text and the linked documentation are the

24
00:01:54,092 --> 00:01:54,676
reference.

25
00:01:55,016 --> 00:02:00,286
GitHub-side labs run in your normal shell,
not in labs/shell, because the lab shell

26
00:02:00,326 --> 00:02:04,893
switches off the system configuration
where the credential helper lives.

27
00:02:05,233 --> 00:02:10,623
The demos use prompt-registry, a small
Python package that stores versioned prompt

28
00:02:10,663 --> 00:02:16,120
templates for an LLM application. The same
files become your practice repository in

29
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:16,716
Lab 19.1.

30
00:02:17,056 --> 00:02:22,146
The local commands in this video are 🟢
SAFE: git clone, git clone --mirror, and

31
00:02:22,186 --> 00:02:23,380
commands that read.

32
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:26,632
After this video you can:

33
00:02:26,972 --> 00:02:31,904
Decide for any item on a repository page
whether it is Git data or a GitHub object,

34
00:02:31,944 --> 00:02:33,462
and prove it from a clone.

35
00:02:33,802 --> 00:02:37,134
Say what a mirror clone carries and
what it leaves on the platform.

36
00:02:37,474 --> 00:02:41,400
Distinguish personal accounts,
organizations and enterprise accounts.

37
00:02:41,740 --> 00:02:46,013
Compute a member's effective access from
base permission, team roles and direct

38
00:02:46,053 --> 00:02:46,395
grants.

39
00:02:46,735 --> 00:02:51,152
Name the repository settings the section
singles out and what each changes.

40
00:02:52,992 --> 00:02:57,670
In one sentence: a repository on GitHub
is a Git repository, which you can copy

41
00:02:57,710 --> 00:03:02,567
completely, surrounded by records in GitHub's
database, which you cannot copy with

42
00:03:02,607 --> 00:03:03,224
Git at all.

43
00:03:09,564 --> 00:03:14,976
Git data first. Commits, trees, file contents
and annotated tag objects are objects.

44
00:03:15,016 --> 00:03:19,194
You read them with git cat-file
-p, and they arrive with a clone.

45
00:03:19,234 --> 00:03:24,516
Branches and tags are refs. You read them
with git for-each-ref and git ls-remote,

46
00:03:24,556 --> 00:03:29,448
and branches arrive as remote-tracking
branches. Author, committer, message,

47
00:03:29,488 --> 00:03:32,823
trailers and signature are
inside the commit object.

48
00:03:33,163 --> 00:03:36,692
A middle category: Git data
that GitHub interprets.

49
00:03:36,732 --> 00:03:42,430
README, LICENSE, and the files under
.github/, issue forms, templates, CODEOWNERS,

50
00:03:42,470 --> 00:03:48,098
workflow files, are tracked files. And
Fixes #12 in a message is text in a commit

51
00:03:48,138 --> 00:03:51,387
object. To Git it's two
words. GitHub reads it.

52
00:03:51,727 --> 00:03:56,357
Git refs that GitHub creates: pull request
head refs, refs/pull/N/head on the

53
00:03:56,397 --> 00:03:59,754
server, read-only. You can
list them with git ls-remote.

54
00:03:59,794 --> 00:04:04,606
They don't arrive with a clone, because
they're outside the default refspec, the

55
00:04:04,646 --> 00:04:07,336
rule that decides which
refs a fetch touches.

56
00:04:07,676 --> 00:04:13,061
GitHub objects, in GitHub's database. A
pull request's title, description, reviews,

57
00:04:13,101 --> 00:04:18,487
comments, checks and merge state. Issues,
sub-issues, labels, milestones, projects,

58
00:04:18,527 --> 00:04:23,717
discussions. A release: its title, notes,
assets, and its draft, pre-release and

59
00:04:23,757 --> 00:04:27,900
latest flags. The tag arrives with
a clone, the release doesn't.

60
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:32,215
The "Verified" badge, and which
account a commit is attributed to.

61
00:04:32,255 --> 00:04:34,960
The fork relationship, stars and watchers.

62
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:40,621
Roles, teams, rulesets, classic branch
protection, secrets, variables, webhooks,

63
00:04:40,661 --> 00:04:44,842
deploy keys and settings. None
of these arrives with git clone.

64
00:04:45,182 --> 00:04:50,564
Three special cases. The wiki is Git, in
a second repository with its own URL: a

65
00:04:50,604 --> 00:04:56,121
separate clone. Packages, workflow runs,
logs, artifacts and caches live in GitHub

66
00:04:56,161 --> 00:05:01,542
Packages and GitHub Actions. And with Git
LFS, the pointer files are Git and the

67
00:05:01,582 --> 00:05:03,440
contents are in LFS storage.

68
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:08,842
Inside .git, a clone holds objects, refs,
HEAD, the index, reflogs and config.

69
00:05:08,882 --> 00:05:14,139
There's no file for an issue or a review.
The only traces of the platform are the

70
00:05:14,179 --> 00:05:19,372
URL in remote.origin.url and what a platform
tool wrote into your configuration,

71
00:05:19,412 --> 00:05:21,530
such as a credential helper line.

72
00:05:21,870 --> 00:05:26,491
One more boundary rule, flagged in the
book as "GitHub, not Git": GitHub also

73
00:05:26,531 --> 00:05:31,213
creates Git data on your behalf. Merge
commits, squash commits, tags made by a

74
00:05:31,253 --> 00:05:36,176
release, the test merge of a pull request.
When a commit or ref exists that nobody

75
00:05:36,216 --> 00:05:39,990
created with a Git command, ask
which platform action wrote it.

76
00:05:40,330 --> 00:05:45,908
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Make
two columns, Git data and GitHub object.

77
00:05:45,948 --> 00:05:49,563
Sort these four: a branch,
an issue, a tag, a release.

78
00:05:49,603 --> 00:05:50,240
I'll wait.

79
00:05:55,080 --> 00:06:00,470
A branch and a tag are refs, so Git data.
An issue and a release are GitHub objects.

80
00:06:00,510 --> 00:06:05,706
If the release tripped you, that's normal:
it points at a tag, and people mix the

81
00:06:05,746 --> 00:06:06,158
two up.

82
00:06:06,498 --> 00:06:11,504
Now accounts. In one sentence: people sign
in to user accounts, and organizations

83
00:06:11,544 --> 00:06:16,488
and enterprises are containers that own
repositories and decide who may do what.

84
00:06:16,528 --> 00:06:21,721
A personal account is one person, on GitHub
Free or GitHub Pro, with an access model

85
00:06:21,761 --> 00:06:26,456
of owner plus collaborators: two levels.
Nobody signs in to an organization.

86
00:06:26,496 --> 00:06:31,502
Members act through their user accounts,
and the organization has five repository

87
00:06:31,542 --> 00:06:34,866
roles, teams, base permissions
and organization roles.

88
00:06:34,906 --> 00:06:38,978
An enterprise owns organizations,
with central policy and billing.

89
00:06:39,318 --> 00:06:43,261
The plan decides which features
work on private repositories.

90
00:06:43,301 --> 00:06:48,550
Public repositories get the full feature
set on every plan, which is why the labs

91
00:06:48,590 --> 00:06:54,035
use public repositories in a free organization.
Organization-level rulesets need the

92
00:06:54,075 --> 00:06:58,083
Team plan. A ruleset is a named
list of rules for chosen refs.

93
00:06:58,123 --> 00:07:03,437
SAML single sign-on, custom roles and
internal repositories need Enterprise Cloud.

94
00:07:03,777 --> 00:07:08,735
Now roles. In one sentence: in an organization,
what a person can do in a repository

95
00:07:08,775 --> 00:07:13,198
is the highest of every grant that
reaches them: the organization-wide base

96
00:07:13,238 --> 00:07:17,601
permission, the roles of their teams,
a direct grant, and ownership of the

97
00:07:17,641 --> 00:07:18,375
organization.

98
00:07:18,715 --> 00:07:24,031
The five repository roles, from least to
most: Read, Triage, Write, Maintain, Admin.

99
00:07:24,071 --> 00:07:28,049
Read can clone, fork, open issues,
comment and submit a review.

100
00:07:28,089 --> 00:07:32,257
Triage adds labels, closing and
assigning, and requesting reviews.

101
00:07:32,597 --> 00:07:37,281
Write adds pushing, merging a pull request,
giving an approval that counts toward

102
00:07:37,321 --> 00:07:42,005
required reviews, acting as a code owner,
creating releases, and creating Actions

103
00:07:42,045 --> 00:07:43,288
secrets and variables.

104
00:07:43,628 --> 00:07:48,628
Maintain adds configuring pull request
merges and pushing to branches under classic

105
00:07:48,668 --> 00:07:53,669
protection. Admin adds rulesets and branch
protection, visibility, access, webhooks

106
00:07:53,709 --> 00:07:56,706
and deploy keys, and archive,
transfer and delete.

107
00:07:57,046 --> 00:08:01,739
Three rows surprise people. Write can
create Actions secrets and edit workflow

108
00:08:01,779 --> 00:08:05,865
files, so Write is enough to run
code with the repository's secrets.

109
00:08:05,905 --> 00:08:10,841
The Maintain role's right to push to
protected branches doesn't apply to rulesets,

110
00:08:10,881 --> 00:08:14,482
which "have a different bypass
model" in the matrix's words.

111
00:08:14,522 --> 00:08:19,276
And Read includes submitting a review,
although only a review from someone with

112
00:08:19,316 --> 00:08:21,339
Write counts toward a requirement.

113
00:08:21,679 --> 00:08:26,357
The base permission is a role that every
member has on every repository of the

114
00:08:26,397 --> 00:08:31,258
organization. The textbook marks the
combined "highest wins" rule as an inference

115
00:08:31,298 --> 00:08:34,464
from GitHub's pages, not a
sentence GitHub publishes.

116
00:08:34,504 --> 00:08:35,613
I keep that caveat.

117
00:08:35,953 --> 00:08:40,692
Visibility and settings. Visibility
decides who can read a repository at all:

118
00:08:40,732 --> 00:08:45,036
public, private, or internal for
organizations owned by an enterprise.

119
00:08:45,076 --> 00:08:50,126
Changing it has documented side effects.
Public to private: stars and watchers are

120
00:08:50,166 --> 00:08:54,780
erased, and public forks stay public,
detached into a network of their own.

121
00:08:55,120 --> 00:09:00,288
Private to public: everyone can read the
code, the complete history, and the Actions

122
00:09:00,328 --> 00:09:04,999
history and logs. Push rulesets are
disabled. Stars and watchers are erased.

123
00:09:05,039 --> 00:09:09,958
Neither direction changes a Git object.
Making a repository private doesn't take

124
00:09:09,998 --> 00:09:13,120
back what was cloned or
forked while it was public.

125
00:09:13,460 --> 00:09:18,860
Then the settings a professional sets on
purpose. Each is a GitHub object, none is

126
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:22,376
in a clone, and none is
restored by pushing a mirror.

127
00:09:22,416 --> 00:09:27,152
The default branch. The features
issues, wiki, discussions and projects.

128
00:09:27,192 --> 00:09:32,658
Pull request access, which since the
thirteenth of February 2026 can be disabled or

129
00:09:32,698 --> 00:09:36,107
limited to collaborators.
The allowed merge methods.

130
00:09:36,147 --> 00:09:39,822
Automatic deletion of head
branches. The forking policy.

131
00:09:39,862 --> 00:09:40,618
And archive.

132
00:09:42,458 --> 00:09:45,895
The textbook's analogy: a
manuscript in a publisher's office.

133
00:09:45,935 --> 00:09:49,713
You can carry a perfect copy of the
manuscript out of the building.

134
00:09:49,753 --> 00:09:54,444
The reviewers' notes, the list of who may
edit it and the approval stamps belong to

135
00:09:54,484 --> 00:09:55,983
the office's filing system.

136
00:09:56,323 --> 00:10:01,384
The analogy breaks in two places. The
office edits your manuscript when asked: a

137
00:10:01,424 --> 00:10:06,614
merge button creates commits. And the office
reads instructions written inside the

138
00:10:06,654 --> 00:10:11,588
manuscript: files under .github/ and
words such as Fixes #12 are Git data that

139
00:10:11,628 --> 00:10:12,736
GitHub interprets.

140
00:10:13,076 --> 00:10:16,627
For access, the analogy is key
cards in an office building.

141
00:10:16,667 --> 00:10:21,739
One rule opens some doors for every employee,
your department's card opens more, and

142
00:10:21,779 --> 00:10:25,148
a card issued to you personally
may open one extra room.

143
00:10:25,188 --> 00:10:30,077
A door opens if any card you hold opens
it. That analogy breaks because buildings

144
00:10:30,117 --> 00:10:35,068
have blacklists and GitHub has none: no
grant can reduce what another grant gives.

145
00:10:36,908 --> 00:10:41,583
The outer box is GitHub. Inside it is
a database, and inside it a bare Git

146
00:10:41,623 --> 00:10:46,870
repository, one with no working tree. The
arrows at the bottom are the only way Git

147
00:10:46,910 --> 00:10:50,820
data moves: clone, fetch, push.
They touch the inner box only.

148
00:10:50,860 --> 00:10:56,108
The arrow on the right is GitHub reading
.github/ and messages out of the Git data.

149
00:10:56,448 --> 00:11:01,076
Quick quiz. Meera joins an organization
whose base permission is Read.

150
00:11:01,116 --> 00:11:06,543
Her team has Write on one repository. On
another, private repository, what can she

151
00:11:06,583 --> 00:11:09,477
do: nothing, Read or
Write? Say it out loud.

152
00:11:14,317 --> 00:11:17,585
Read each column from top to
bottom and take the highest.

153
00:11:17,625 --> 00:11:21,473
On the right, the only grant is the
base permission, and it's Read.

154
00:11:21,513 --> 00:11:26,174
That's the answer to the CTO's second
question. If you said nothing, you reasoned

155
00:11:26,214 --> 00:11:28,031
the way the access was designed.

156
00:11:28,371 --> 00:11:33,297
Read the line on why GitHub does it: grants
only add, the highest grant wins, and

157
00:11:33,337 --> 00:11:34,400
nothing subtracts.

158
00:11:38,740 --> 00:11:42,125
How many refs will the clone
have, and of which kinds?

159
00:11:42,165 --> 00:11:43,837
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

160
00:11:48,677 --> 00:11:53,540
Five refs, each naming an object. With
the objects reachable from them, that is

161
00:11:53,580 --> 00:11:55,402
everything the clone received.

162
00:11:55,742 --> 00:11:59,813
The files that configure the platform came
along because they are tracked files.

163
00:12:00,153 --> 00:12:06,379
To Git, Fixes #12 is two words in a message.
No issue 12 exists in this sandbox.

164
00:12:06,719 --> 00:12:11,688
A mirror asks for every ref the server
offers. What does it receive beyond refs and

165
00:12:11,728 --> 00:12:13,498
objects? Make your prediction.

166
00:12:23,838 --> 00:12:28,816
Nothing. Three refs, 33 objects. Against
GitHub a mirror also receives the

167
00:12:28,856 --> 00:12:32,816
refs/pull/* refs, and no issue,
review, release or setting.

168
00:12:32,856 --> 00:12:35,257
That's Monday morning from the hook.

169
00:12:38,097 --> 00:12:42,229
Your local configuration and your
reflog. No clone receives them.

170
00:12:42,569 --> 00:12:47,681
Open your practice repository's page in the
browser and walk it from top to bottom.

171
00:12:47,721 --> 00:12:52,213
For each element, say the column. The
file list and the README: Git data.

172
00:12:52,253 --> 00:12:56,621
The branch selector and the tag count:
refs. The commit count: objects.

173
00:12:56,661 --> 00:13:01,649
The star and watch counters: database. The
issue and pull request tabs: database.

174
00:13:01,689 --> 00:13:06,678
The "About" description and topics: settings.
The releases panel: a GitHub object

175
00:13:06,718 --> 00:13:07,795
pointing at a tag.

176
00:13:08,135 --> 00:13:10,915
Then prove it from the terminal
with the commands of the lab.

177
00:13:11,255 --> 00:13:16,522
The rule you're demonstrating: what git
ls-remote can show is Git data, and what

178
00:13:16,562 --> 00:13:22,093
only gh can show is a GitHub object. The
lab also has you create one test issue with

179
00:13:22,133 --> 00:13:26,604
gh issue create. Do that in your
own repository when you do the lab.

180
00:13:28,444 --> 00:13:30,092
Five mistakes to watch for.

181
00:13:30,432 --> 00:13:34,697
Treating git clone --mirror as a
backup of the repository page.

182
00:13:34,737 --> 00:13:40,232
Root cause: a mirror receives refs and
objects; issues, reviews, releases, rules,

183
00:13:40,272 --> 00:13:42,624
roles and secrets are not Git data.

184
00:13:42,964 --> 00:13:47,354
Granting Write on one repository and
assuming the person sees nothing else.

185
00:13:47,394 --> 00:13:52,316
Root cause: the base permission applies to
every member on every repository, and the

186
00:13:52,356 --> 00:13:53,438
highest grant wins.

187
00:13:53,778 --> 00:13:56,985
Giving Write to someone who
must not reach secrets.

188
00:13:57,025 --> 00:14:01,950
Root cause: Write can edit workflow files
and create Actions secrets, so it is

189
00:14:01,990 --> 00:14:05,069
enough to run code with
the repository's secrets.

190
00:14:05,409 --> 00:14:10,255
Making a repository private to take back
a leak. Root cause: visibility changes no

191
00:14:10,295 --> 00:14:13,651
Git object and does not recall
what was cloned or forked.

192
00:14:13,991 --> 00:14:18,309
Expecting settings to return after
pushing a mirror to a new repository.

193
00:14:18,349 --> 00:14:21,033
Root cause: every setting
is a GitHub object.

194
00:14:22,873 --> 00:14:28,012
Now, out of the lab. A startup's code lives
under the founder's personal account,

195
00:14:28,052 --> 00:14:33,191
with ten collaborators. Every collaborator
can push to everything they were added

196
00:14:33,231 --> 00:14:38,242
to, there are no teams, and the company's
access model is one person's account.

197
00:14:38,282 --> 00:14:40,672
The fix is to move to an organization.

198
00:14:41,012 --> 00:14:46,190
Transferring a repository keeps its Git
data, issues, pull requests, wiki, stars and

199
00:14:46,230 --> 00:14:50,291
watchers, and its webhooks, secrets
and deploy keys stay attached.

200
00:14:50,331 --> 00:14:55,385
What the company gains is the access model
of this video: a base permission chosen

201
00:14:55,425 --> 00:14:58,553
on purpose, and repositories
granted through teams.

202
00:14:58,893 --> 00:15:03,612
One warning from the same section for
the day someone leaves: deploy keys are

203
00:15:03,652 --> 00:15:08,742
outside this model. A deploy key is an SSH
key attached to one repository, not to a

204
00:15:08,782 --> 00:15:13,562
person. Anyone holding a deploy key's
private half can use it, in the words of

205
00:15:13,602 --> 00:15:18,198
GitHub's roles page, "even if they're
later removed from the organization".

206
00:15:18,238 --> 00:15:22,338
Offboarding a person doesn't offboard
the credentials they created.

207
00:15:24,178 --> 00:15:29,005
Your turn. Do Lab 19.2, "An inventory
of Git data and GitHub objects", in

208
00:15:29,045 --> 00:15:34,205
lab-manual/m19-github-platform.md. The
lab gives you a table of twelve things.

209
00:15:34,245 --> 00:15:39,471
Fill in the layer and the mirror column
for every row on paper before you run a

210
00:15:39,511 --> 00:15:43,405
single command. Then find the
command that proves each row.

211
00:15:43,745 --> 00:15:51,733
The challenge is Exercise 19.3, "Effective
access", in exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

